      <h2 id="who">Who we are</h2>
      <p>
        Rosie is an automated AI booking assistant run by Rosfield. You may book with a business through Rosie by text,
        the booking page or your own AI assistant. Rosfield is paid by the business, never by you.
      </p>
      <p>Rosfield is run by <span class="placeholder">[legal name of the person or company: to be set]</span>.</p>

      <h2 id="collect">What we collect, and why</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Your name, mobile number, and email if you give one. Why: to make and manage your booking and reach you.
        </li>
        <li>
          The booking: the service, date, time, place, price, and what you agreed to, with the words we showed you.
        </li>
        <li>Your messages with Rosie. Why: to answer you and keep a record of what you agreed.</li>
        <li>Payment status. Card and bank details go to Stripe, never to us.</li>
        <li>Codes and proof that a request came from you, and the record of your yes to texts, plans and fees.</li>
        <li>
          The visit check: the day after a booking with no check-in, we may ask once if your visit happened. Why: to
          check the fee count and flag disputes. We use it for nothing else.
        </li>
        <li>Private details (see <a href="#sensitive">Sensitive details</a> below) only on the secure page.</li>
        <li>
          For business owners and staff who connect a calendar: busy times only, as set out in
          <a href="#google">Google calendar data</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          On rosfield.com: the pages you visit and how you found them, through Google Analytics. Why: to count visits
          and improve the site. See <a href="#analytics">Website analytics</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="share">Who gets it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The business you booked. It sees your booking. It uses your data only for itself.</li>
        <li>
          Service providers that run Rosie, each for its own job: Stripe (payments), Cloudflare (hosting and storage),
          Anthropic (the AI model that writes Rosie's replies), Linq (iMessage), Resend (email),
          <span class="placeholder">[map and distance provider: not yet chosen]</span>,
          <span class="placeholder">[owner ID and face check provider: not yet chosen]</span>, and
          <span class="placeholder">[SMS provider: not yet chosen]</span> (text messages).
        </li>
        <li>Google, for website analytics on rosfield.com (see <a href="#analytics">Website analytics</a>).</li>
        <li>
          We do not sell your data, share it for ads, use it to train AI, or join it across businesses. We pass a court
          order or police request to the business where the law allows.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="sms">Text messages and your mobile number</h2>
      <p>
        <strong
          >We do not share or sell your mobile number or your text-message opt-in data and consent to any third party or
          affiliate for marketing or promotional purposes.</strong
        >
        The categories of sharing above exclude text-message opt-in data and consent: this information is not shared
        with any third party. The providers that carry Rosie's messages (Linq for iMessage and the SMS provider) receive
        your number only to deliver Rosie's messages to you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rosie texts only a number given for a booking, a number that joined a business's team, or a number that said yes
        on a forwarded link. Reply STOP to stop Rosie's texts and HELP for help. Message frequency, rates and the rest
        are in the <a href="/sms">SMS terms</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="google">Google calendar data</h2>
      <p>
        A business owner or staff member can connect a Google calendar so Rosie does not offer times when they are busy.
        This section covers the information Rosie receives from Google APIs.
      </p>
      <h3>What Rosie accesses</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Free and busy times</strong> (<code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.freebusy</code>): when
          you are busy, never what your events are. Rosie cannot see event titles, descriptions, guests or places.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Its own bookings calendar</strong>
          (<code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.app.created</code>): Rosie creates one calendar called "Rosie
          bookings" and adds, moves and removes the bookings it makes there. It cannot read or change your other
          calendars.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>How Rosie uses it</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>To offer customers only times when you are free, together with your hours and the bookings Rosie holds.</li>
        <li>To keep the bookings Rosie makes for you visible in your own Google calendar.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>How it is stored and protected</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The permission Google gives Rosie (a refresh token) is stored encrypted. The short-lived access tokens are
          never stored.
        </li>
        <li>Data moves over encrypted connections and is held on Cloudflare's infrastructure.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Who it is shared with</h3>
      <p>
        Google calendar data is not sold, not shared for ads, not used to train AI models, and not transferred to anyone
        except as needed to run the calendar feature for you or to comply with the law. People at Rosfield do not read
        it, except with your permission, for security, or to comply with the law.
      </p>
      <h3>Limited Use</h3>
      <p>
        <strong
          >Rosfield's use and transfer to any other app of information received from Google APIs will adhere to the
          <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
            >Google API Services User Data Policy</a
          >, including the Limited Use requirements.</strong
        >
      </p>
      <h3>Removing access</h3>
      <p>
        You can remove Rosie's access at any time in your
        <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">Google Account settings</a>, or ask Rosie in the chat. Once
        access is removed, Rosie can no longer read your busy times or write to its bookings calendar, and it stops
        offering times for you until you connect again. Bookings already made stay. To ask that we delete the stored
        permission and related records, write to <span class="placeholder">[privacy email: to be set]</span>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Microsoft calendars work the same way: Rosie reads only the start, end and busy status of events and writes only
        its own bookings. Apple Calendar gets a one-way feed of Rosie's bookings and sends nothing back.
      </p>

      <h2 id="analytics">Website analytics</h2>
      <p>
        rosfield.com uses Google Analytics, a Google service, to count visits and see how people use the site. It sets
        cookies in your browser (the <code>_ga</code> cookies) and records the pages you visit, how you got here, your
        device and browser, and your rough location, worked out from your IP address, which Google Analytics does not
        store. We see counts and anonymous visit records, never who you are. We never send Google your name, number,
        email or what you write to Rosie.
      </p>
      <p>
        We use it only to improve the site, never for ads. Visit records are kept for up to 14 months; after that only
        totals remain. Google's own use is set out in
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites"
          >How Google uses information from sites or apps that use our services</a
        >. To stop Google Analytics on every site you visit, install the
        <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout">Google Analytics opt-out add-on</a>. Blocking cookies for
        rosfield.com stops it from recognizing you on return visits.
      </p>

